<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carrowmore</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carrowmore megalithic cemetery</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carrowmore is a large cemetery of megalithic tombs on the Cúil Irra peninsula near Sligo in north-west Ireland. Its dozens of passage tombs and stone circles, some dated to the 4th millennium BCE, are among the oldest in Ireland. It lies below Knocknarea, whose summit cairn is linked by legend with Queen Medb.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carrowmore.jpg" alt="Carrowmore" width="320" height="181" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas F. Borchert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carrowmore_Megalithic_Cemetery_P7_2015_09_08.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Carrowmore, in Irish An Cheathrú Mhór, the great quarter, stands on a small plateau between 36.5 and 59 metres above sea level, at the centre of a ritual landscape dominated to the west by Knocknarea and the great cairn of Miosgán Médhbh on its summit. Thirty tombs survive and another 25 have been destroyed since 1800, many by quarrying and field clearance. With Carrowkeel, Loughcrew and Brú na Bóinne it is one of the big four clusters of megalithic tombs in Ireland.</p><p>Most tombs are satellites of the largest monument, the restored cairn of Listoghil, or Tomb 51, erected c. 3500 BCE and 34 metres across. Each satellite was a small dolmen of five upright stones under a capstone, ringed by 30 to 40 boulders, usually of gneiss, and most face the central area. The burials were mostly cremations of several people, with antler and bone pins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a>, pendants and quartz fragments, and the chambers were reused in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze">Bronze</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Ages</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Gabriel Beranger drew the monuments in 1779, George Petrie surveyed and numbered them for the Ordnance Survey of 1837, and William Gregory Wood-Martin made the first recorded excavations in the 1880s, after the landlord Rodger Walker had dug there for antiquities. The Swedish archaeologist Göran Burenhult, who excavated in 1977–1982 and 1994–1998, read his radiocarbon dates as showing that hunter-gatherers built the tombs, but a later review with 25 new dates placed them between about 3750 and 3000 BCE. Ancient DNA showed a man buried in Listoghil to be related to people buried at Newgrange, Millin Bay and Carrowkeel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Carrowmore is a protected National Monument. A plan of 1983 by Sligo County Council for a landfill dump near the complex was rejected by the Supreme Court in 1989, the first explicit legal recognition of an architectural landscape. Most of the site is now in public ownership, with a visitor centre in a farmhouse near the R292 road.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Carrowmore” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The region was rich in older monuments such as the <b>Carrowmore</b> tombs.</blockquote><a href="/state/nagnatae">Nagnatae</a></li></ul></section>
